  "title": "Variables and print",
  "summary": "Store values under names and show them on screen.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "intro": {
    "text": "A program needs to remember things: a name, a score, a price. A **variable** is how it remembers. You pick a name, write `=`, then the value: `score = 10`. Read `=` as \"gets\": score gets 10.\n\nText goes inside quotes, like `\"Lima\"`. Whole numbers have no quotes, like `18`. To show something on screen, use `print(...)`.\n\nNames can use letters, digits and `_`, but cannot start with a digit. Case matters: `score` and `Score` are two different names.",
    "analogy": "Think of a variable as a sticky label on a jar. The label is the name, the jar holds the value. You can move the label to a new jar any time, but one label is only ever on one jar."
  },
